{"title":"Japanese Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Japanese Theorem</b> is a theorem which holds for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cyclic_polygon\" title=\"Cyclic polygon\">cyclic polygons</a>.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Japanese_Theorem_for_cyclic_quadrilaterals\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Japanese Theorem for cyclic quadrilaterals</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Statement_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Japanese_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For any triangulated cyclic polygon, the sum of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius\" title=\"Inradius\">inradii</a> of the triangles is constant.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Japanese_Theorem_for_cyclic_quadrilaterals\">Japanese Theorem for cyclic quadrilaterals</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Japanese_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Japanese Theorem for cyclic quadrilaterals\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement_2\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Japanese_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The <b>Japanese theorem for cyclic quadrilaterals</b> states that for a cyclic quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Incenter\" title=\"Incenter\">incenters</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/6/3c6a4a0680d4e0668219585d856c7f109cd09f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/7/c879631d01bcc51ec112e4a32a039f624d40c91c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/7/3e7f612d9927fd497991d0f6bf37768439ba47fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b0f83f18342a9acbda51a6899ef8fc7ac9dfea3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" /> of triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/f/12f372757d55c2d07adc720932c6d6e1396aa389.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/7/0f7a55113a84ee4c04eb66f66d08310c32533ca2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ACD$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/0/1a01619f6185acc5fd837a1296335dd7338582b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_1M_2M_3M_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"99\" height=\"15\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rectangle\" title=\"Rectangle\">rectangle</a>.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-left\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9e/Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png/300px-Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"303\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/9/9e/Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png/450px-Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/9/9e/Japanese_theorem_quadrilaterals.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Japanese_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, we can see that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94befad0f73db7a2c776f5853731cd449f4d6434.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BM_2C = 90^{\\circ} + \\frac{1}{2} \\angle CAB\\]\" width=\"206\" height=\"37\" />\nand similarly, from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/f/12f372757d55c2d07adc720932c6d6e1396aa389.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" /> we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/3/2233362494106d3672dbeaad1c99d5f393ddabf7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BM_3C = 90^{\\circ} + \\frac{1}{2} \\angle CDB\\]\" width=\"208\" height=\"37\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is cyclic, therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/2/b225937d85de32f0c68ef69dbacd02c3b73cb752.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CDB = \\angle CAB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"139\" height=\"14\" />, which means that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8cc28d63743b65ef121c81d48f41116a025b1d29.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BM_2C = \\angle BM_3C\\]\" width=\"157\" height=\"15\" />\nFrom this, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b767084c9c1b4a72be7927ee7d2c0733f4019b90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BM_2M_3C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"17\" /> is cyclic. This means that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/4/624ae048c62b8b40210340265b290cab42d65fc9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BCM_3 + \\angle BM_2M_3 = 180^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"223\" height=\"16\" />\nBy symmetry, we can also derive\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/e/9fe99b2b3601936aea91ec7fed55c01a93377fe6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BAM_1 + \\angle BM_2M_1 = 180^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"222\" height=\"16\" />\nAdding these equations, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/1/d5145ebb5b5a981d316ec5e9e775f6465645359b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BAM_1 + \\angle BCM_3 + \\angle BM_2M_1 + \\angle BM_2M_3 = 360^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"412\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/a/3baacf9636c388fc1ef4605b7ba020729931a9b5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies \\angle BM_2M_1 + \\angle BM_2M_3 = 360^{\\circ} - \\angle BAM_1 - \\angle BCM_3 = 360^{\\circ} - \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\angle CAB + \\angle CDB \\right)\\]\" width=\"665\" height=\"37\" />\nWhich implies\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/9/4796c7ac21748d44a8409f60dc2a3b9327d22cd3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[M_1M_2M_3 = 90^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"123\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>And other angles similarly. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Japanese_Theorem&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018092102\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.024 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.044 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 117/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 289/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4509/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.868      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.868      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:17718:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018092102 and revision id 246547. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}